<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- com.murakumo.sysctl-baseline — kernel network baseline for a fleet node.
     Rendered per-node by `murakumo provision` and loaded as a root
     LaunchDaemon; runs once at boot (RunAtLoad) and again on every
     re-provision. macOS has no /etc/sysctl.conf, so this IS the persistence.

     Why (2026-09-02, ADR-260902-fleet-outbound-hygiene): a node's default
     ephemeral range is 16,384 ports (49152-65535) or 32,768 after Big Sur
     (32768-65535). A resident poller with no backoff plus a kernel that had
     stopped reaping TIME_WAIT after 60 days of uptime filled that range on
     benjamin and simeon; every outbound connect then failed with
     EADDRNOTAVAIL, including the mesh watchdog's own health probe.
     Lowering `first` to 16384 gives 49,152 ports — headroom, not a cure: the
     cure is the poller's backoff. The `ops status` PORTS column shows the
     pressure.

     Amended 2026-09-09. "For stuck TIME_WAIT, a reboot" was too strong, and
     believing it kept three nodes dark for weeks behind a reboot FileVault
     would not let them finish. A reboot is what RECLAIMS the wedged sockets;
     it is not what RESTORES SERVICE. Moving `first` BELOW the wedged block
     does that immediately, because the stuck entries occupy a contiguous
     range above the old floor. Measured that day: benjamin and simeon were at
     first=16384 with 48,298 and 48,907 wedged (98% and 99.5%); setting
     first=12000 gave 5,238 and 4,629 free ports and both rejoined the pool
     within minutes, with no reboot. joseph had never received this daemon at
     all and sat on the macOS default 49152 with 15,763 of its 16,384 stuck;
     installing the baseline alone freed 33,389.

     Two things were also measured NOT to work, so nobody repeats them:
     lowering net.inet.tcp.msl (15000 -> 1000) does not expire the wedged
     entries — benjamin's count was identical 45 s later — and neither does
     stopping the producer.

     The floor may only be lowered past a node's own fixed listeners. On these
     minis the highest below the old floor is ollama at 11434, which is why
     12000 is the value and not something rounder. -->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>            <string>com.murakumo.sysctl-baseline</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/sbin/sysctl</string>
    <string>-w</string>
    <string>net.inet.ip.portrange.first=16384</string>
    <string>net.inet.ip.portrange.hifirst=16384</string>
  </array>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>        <true/>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>  <string>{{HOME}}/.murakumo/sysctl-baseline.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>{{HOME}}/.murakumo/sysctl-baseline.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
